DM: You're all in a cage. Bars surround you, making up the walls, floor and ceiling. The cage is suspended above an immeasurable fall, held up by a single chain. Enclosed with you in the 20ft. cube is a menacing druid, fast asleep in his bear form. Your cage is the only one in the room, an additional 20ft. from the walls and ceiling. In the dim lighting, you can barely make out the shape of an arch, beyond which is a magical darkness no eyes can pirce. You have been stripped of your memories and all you have is your starter gear, two 15ft. long iron poles, and a silver clasp.
The bars on the ceiling and floor, the chain, and the clasp are all mithril, and as such are unbreakable. The bars themselves are usable weapons, with a to-hit bonus of +1+PB+Strength modifier, dealing 1d4+to-hit bonus Bludgeoning damage on a hit and forcing the target to make both Constitution and Dexterity saving throws, dealing an additional 4d4 blugeoning damage on two failed saves, half as much on split saves, or none on two succesful throws, with the target knocked prone if the Dexterity throw is failed. The bars that make up the walls are enchanted silver, and they break upon a 20+ srength check or an 11+ to hit roll with a mithril object. Over the course of the entire campaign, unless otherwise stated in the DM-only notes after the description of the room, the druid revives all characters ASAP. As DM, it is up to you how detailed the druid's stat block/character sheet  is, along with their pronouns, but please note that they have an unlimited ability to revive characters, except after three failed death saves, which is a scnario that will be explaind later. After the room is cleared, tell each player IN PRIVATE how many times they leveled up, but not why. They will update their character sheet in the break taken in between rooms during the short rest.
